# Commit 3: Local Integration Process / ProcessCall wiring fix + ContentModifier validation

**Branch**: `Work-on-Parameters-and-Remaining-Components`
**Scope**: `packages/compiler` (the `@cpi-ai/compiler` package)
**Audience**: same as [`commit1_jdbc-support-and-naming-fixes.readme`](commit1_jdbc-support-and-naming-fixes.readme)
and [`commit2_process-direct-support.readme`](commit2_process-direct-support.readme) -- written for both
future Claude sessions and human contributors, explaining the evidence and
reasoning behind each decision.

---

## 1. What broke, and why (reported via a real generated ZIP)

A user-generated `.iflw` (`HTTPS_to_ProcessDirect_Flow.iflw`) failed to
import into SAP with two problems:

1. **"The assigned Local Integration Process does not exist"** on a
   `ProcessCall` step ("Call Order Process").
2. **"Prepare Order Data"** (a Content Modifier) rendered as SAP's generic
   palette description instead of actually running.

### 1a. Root cause: Local Integration Process was modeled but never mapped

`src/model/LocalIntegrationProcess.ts` and `IFlow.addSubProcess()` /
`getSubProcesses()` already existed, and `ComponentFactory.fromJson()`'s
`subProcesses` JSON array already built `LocalIntegrationProcess` instances
from it -- but **`BpmnProcessMapper` never called `flow.getSubProcesses()`
at all**. Confirmed by grep: zero references anywhere in the mapper/writer
layers. So even a caller who correctly declared a `subProcesses` entry got
a `.iflw` with nothing for it -- no sibling `<bpmn2:process>`, no
participant, nothing for a `ProcessCall` to point at.

Separately, there was no mechanism linking a `ProcessCall`'s `processId` to
the actual technical id SAP needs (`Process_<generated>`, an opaque string
the AI/caller has no way to predict ahead of time). The reported ZIP's
`ProcessCall` used `processId: "/process/orders"` -- a value that matches
nothing, because nothing in the pipeline ever tried to resolve it.

**Evidence for the correct BPMN shape** (this compiler's own
`process_direct_reference.zip`, used earlier for the Process Direct
adapter, turns out to also contain a genuine Local Integration Process):

- `Process_49` ("Exception Handling") is a **sibling** `<bpmn2:process>` to
  the main `Process_1`, NOT nested inside it. `processType=directCall`,
  `cmdVariantUri=ctype::FlowElementVariant/cname::LocalIntegrationProcess/version::1.1.3`,
  `componentVersion=1.1`, `transactionalHandling=From Calling Process`.
- Its start/end events (`StartEvent_50`/`EndEvent_51`) are **plain** --
  no `<bpmn2:messageEventDefinition/>`, unlike the main process's
  message-triggered `StartEvent_2`/`EndEvent_2`. Makes sense: a Local
  Integration Process is invoked programmatically via `ProcessCall`, not
  triggered by an adapter message.
- A collaboration participant `Participant_Process_49`
  (`ifl:type="IntegrationProcess"`, `processRef="Process_49"`) references
  it, same shape as the main process's own `Participant_Process_1`.
- The `ProcessCall` (`CallActivity_45`, activityType=ProcessCallElement,
  cmdVariantUri=.../NonLoopingProcess/version::1.0.4) that invokes it lives
  **inside an Exception Subprocess** (`SubProcess_39`), with
  `processId=Process_49` -- an exact string match against the sibling
  process's `id`. This is the literal SAP check behind "The assigned Local
  Integration Process does not exist": if that string doesn't match any
  declared `<bpmn2:process id="...">`, SAP rejects the flow.
- `Process_49` itself contains `ServiceTask_56` -- a Process Direct
  `ExternalCall`, i.e. a mid-flow adapter call can live *inside* a Local
  Integration Process, not just the main process.

### 1b. Root cause: ContentModifier had zero input validation

`ComponentFactory.createComponent('ContentModifier', ...)` passed every
property straight through with no checks. The reported flow had:
`bodyType: "Expression"` (capitalized -- every real SAP export in this
repo uses lowercase `"expression"`/`"constant"`, and SAP is case-sensitive
here) and `wrapContent: false` (a boolean, serialized as the literal
string `"false"`, not real content). The result: a Content Modifier with no
real body configured, which SAP shows with its generic palette description
("Modifies incoming message with additional information") instead of
running.

---

## 2. The fix

### 2a. BpmnProcessMapper now maps every Local Integration Process

New private method `mapLocalIntegrationProcess()` builds, for each entry in
`flow.getSubProcesses()`:
- A sibling `BpmnProcess` (own `<bpmn2:process>`) with the exact evidenced
  property set/order (`transactionTimeout`, `processType=directCall`,
  `componentVersion=1.1`, the `LocalIntegrationProcess/1.1.3` `cmdVariantUri`,
  `transactionalHandling`).
- Plain (non-message) start/end events -- `BpmnNode` gained an
  `isMessageEvent` flag (default `true`, so every existing call site is
  unaffected) and `EventWriter` now only emits
  `<bpmn2:messageEventDefinition/>` when it's true.
- Its own components, mapped via the same `ComponentMapper` the main
  process uses -- so a Local Integration Process supports everything the
  main process does (Router, Multicast, JdbcCall, ProcessDirectCall,
  nested ProcessCall) for free.
- Its own sequence flows, including Router/Multicast gateway-route
  metadata -- this logic was extracted from the main process's inline
  `connections.forEach(...)` block into a shared `buildSequenceFlow()`
  method so both paths use identical logic without duplicating it. The
  main process's own ID scheme (`SequenceFlow_3`, `SequenceFlow_${n+4}`,
  ...) is completely unchanged; only the *route-metadata* logic is shared.
- A `Participant_LIP_<n>` participant (`ifl:type="IntegrationProcess"`,
  `processRef=<the process id>`).
- Mid-flow adapter calls (JDBC, Process Direct) found among its own
  components -- reusing the existing `mapMidFlowAdapterCall()` via a new
  `mapMidFlowAdapterCalls()` list-wrapper, applied to both the main
  process's components and every Local Integration Process's components.

`BpmnDefinitions` gained an `additionalProcesses: BpmnProcess[]` array
(4th constructor param, defaults to `[]` -- non-breaking), and
`DefinitionsWriter` now writes each as a sibling `<bpmn2:process>` *before*
the main process, matching the order observed in the reference export.

The collaboration diagram (`createDiagram()`) was refactored so the main
process's node/shape/edge logic lives in a new `addProcessNodesAndFlows()`
method parameterized by an `(originX, originY)` origin, reused for the main
process (origin `(250, 60)`, producing byte-identical output to before --
verified) and for each Local Integration Process (a new lane stacked below
the main one). Any `ifl:type="IntegrationProcess"` participant other than
`Participant_Process_1` now gets the same full-size lane box treatment.

### 2b. ComponentFactory resolves ProcessCall.processId automatically

`IFlowJson.subProcesses[]` entries gained an optional `id` field -- an
AI-chosen logical reference key, exactly like `components[].id`. `fromJson()`
now runs a pre-pass that creates `LocalIntegrationProcess` "shells" (id/name
only) for every declared `subProcess` *before* any component is created,
building a `subProcessMap` keyed by `id` (or `name` if no `id` given). A new
`resolveProcessCallProcessId()` helper rewrites a `ProcessCall`'s
`config.processId` from that logical key to the real generated technical
id, applied at all three places a `ProcessCall` can legally appear: main
`components`, another `subProcess`'s components, and an
`exceptionSubprocess`'s components (the last one matters because that's
literally where the real evidence places it). The pre-created shells are
reused (not re-constructed) when their own components/connections are
filled in later, so there's never a second, orphaned
`LocalIntegrationProcess` instance per `subDef`.

`LocalIntegrationProcess` itself gained an optional `id?` constructor param
(uses `IdGenerator.next("Process")` instead of the old `Date.now()`
fallback when omitted), matching the pattern already used for
`ProcessCall`/`JdbcCall`/etc. after the CP-001 fix in commit 1.

### 2c. ContentModifier property validation

New `validateContentModifierProperties()` in `ComponentFactory.ts`:
- `bodyType`, if present, is normalized case-insensitively against the two
  evidenced values (`"constant"`, `"expression"`) and rejected if it
  matches neither -- so `"Expression"` (the reported bug) is silently
  *corrected*, but an invented value like `"xml"` is rejected outright.
- `wrapContent` / `propertyTable` / `headerTable`, if present, must be
  strings -- a boolean (the reported bug's other half) now throws a clear
  error instead of being serialized as the literal string `"false"`.

This does not add or remove any supported property -- it validates the
same four properties `ComponentRegistry.Enricher.metadata.defaultProperties`
already documents, and `ComponentMapper`'s existing default-merging
behavior (fills in anything left unset) is untouched.

---

## 3. Validation hardening (`validate.ts`)

- **PC-001** -- a `ProcessCall`'s `processId` must match the `id` of a
  `LocalIntegrationProcess` declared on the flow (`flow.getSubProcesses()`).
  Checked across the *whole* flow: main components, every subProcess's own
  components, and every exceptionSubprocess's own components (matching
  where real evidence places it). This is the exact SAP check behind "The
  assigned Local Integration Process does not exist" -- previously
  `validate()` had no opinion on this at all, so `valid: true` could still
  produce a SAP-rejected ZIP.
- **LIP-001** -- every Local Integration Process must have a valid XML
  NCName id (defensive backstop, same rationale as NM-001 -- should be
  unreachable via `fromJson()`).
- **LIP-002** -- Local Integration Process ids must be unique (defensive
  backstop for hand-built `IFlow` usage outside `fromJson()`, which already
  dedupes AI-supplied subProcess ids before this point).

Items already covered by pre-existing behavior, not touched here:
"Connections to nonexistent components" (already a hard throw in
`fromJson()`'s connection-resolution loop) and "Duplicate component IDs"
(already CP-001, from commit 1).

---

## 4. Files changed (this package only)

**New**:
- `test-localintegrationprocess-regression.ts`

**Modified**:
- `src/model/LocalIntegrationProcess.ts` (optional `id` param)
- `src/ir/BpmnNode.ts` (`isMessageEvent` flag)
- `src/ir/BpmnDefinitions.ts` (`additionalProcesses[]`)
- `src/writer/EventWriter.ts` (conditional `messageEventDefinition`)
- `src/writer/DefinitionsWriter.ts` (writes sibling processes)
- `src/mapper/BpmnProcessMapper.ts` (`mapLocalIntegrationProcess()`,
  extracted `buildSequenceFlow()` / `mapMidFlowAdapterCalls()`, diagram
  support for LIP lanes -- verified byte-identical main-process diagram
  output before/after via direct coordinate comparison)
- `src/factory/ComponentFactory.ts` (`subProcesses[].id`, pre-pass +
  `resolveProcessCallProcessId()`, `validateContentModifierProperties()`)
- `src/api/validate.ts` (PC-001, LIP-001, LIP-002)
- `src/api/capabilities.ts` (documents the real `ProcessCall` ↔
  `subProcesses` linkage and the `ContentModifier` schema)
- `test-naming-version-regression.ts`, `test-processdirect-regression.ts`
  (both had a pre-existing `ProcessCall` scenario using a made-up
  `processId` with no matching `subProcess` -- exactly the bug being
  fixed here. Updated to declare a real matching `subProcesses` entry, which
  is itself additional regression coverage for the fix.)

**Deliberately not touched**: `ExceptionSubprocess` → BPMN mapping remains
unmapped (same pre-existing gap as `LocalIntegrationProcess` was, but out of
scope here -- the reported flow doesn't use exception handling, and
`exceptionSubprocesses`' *components* are still resolved/validated
correctly by this fix wherever they contain a `ProcessCall`, only the
subprocess's own BPMN wrapper element is not yet emitted). Writers,
packaging logic, existing resource handling, and every other adapter/
component type are unchanged.

---

## 5. Testing

`test-localintegrationprocess-regression.ts` (same `ts-node` convention as
commits 1-2), 5 scenario groups, all passing, each checked against the raw
generated `.iflw` XML (not just `validate()`'s return value):

1. The exact reported scenario (HTTPS → Prepare Order Data → Call Order
   Process → Create Response → HTTP, with a real Local Integration
   Process) -- verifies the sibling `<bpmn2:process>`, its evidenced
   properties, plain start/end events, the participant, and the exact
   `processId` string match SAP needs, plus the corrected `ContentModifier`
   output.
2. `ProcessCall` inside an `exceptionSubprocess` targeting a sibling Local
   Integration Process (the real evidence's own placement).
3. Multiple Local Integration Processes in one flow -- no duplicate ids
   across 55 total generated XML element ids.
4. PC-001: an unresolvable `ProcessCall` target is rejected, not silently
   accepted.
5. `ContentModifier` rejects an invalid `bodyType` and a wrong-typed
   `wrapContent`; confirms `"Expression"` (wrong case) is normalized
   rather than rejected.

Additionally regenerated the exact originally-reported flow shape end to
end and confirmed directly in the output ZIP: the Local Integration
Process's `<bpmn2:process id="orderProcess">` exists, `Call Order
Process`'s `processId` property equals `"orderProcess"` exactly, and
`Prepare Order Data` carries `bodyType="expression"` /
`wrapContent="${body}"` instead of the broken values.

**Full pre-existing regression suite re-run -- zero regressions**:
`test-jdbc-regression.ts`, `test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts`,
`test-naming-version-regression.ts`, `test-customer-flow-regression.ts`,
`test-processdirect-regression.ts`, `test/run-rt-003-tests.ts` (9/9),
`test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts` (3/3) -- all pass, including a
direct coordinate-by-coordinate check that the main process's diagram
output is unchanged after the `BpmnProcessMapper` refactor.

Run with (from `packages/compiler/`):
```
npm run build   # or: npx tsc --noEmit
npx ts-node test-localintegrationprocess-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-processdirect-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jdbc-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-naming-version-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-customer-flow-regression.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-rt-003-tests.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts
```

---

## 6. Known limitations

- `ExceptionSubprocess` itself (the `<bpmn2:subProcess
  activityType="ErrorEventSubProcessTemplate">` wrapper) is still not
  mapped to BPMN output -- only its *components* are correctly created and
  validated. A `ProcessCall` declared inside one resolves correctly against
  a Local Integration Process, but the exception-handling wrapper around it
  is not yet emitted. Out of scope for this fix (not needed by the reported
  flow); would need the same evidence-based treatment `LocalIntegrationProcess`
  just got.
- Diagram coordinates for multiple Local Integration Process lanes are a
  reasonable non-overlapping placement, not pixel values pulled from a real
  export (evidence shows no fixed layout for this since it varies per
  flow).
